=====================================================================
A. VALGRIND
=====================================================================
"Valgrind isn't an acronym - it's the name of a dynamic binary
instrumentation framework, a suite of tools that run your program on
a synthetic CPU, letting it inspect every memory access. The tool I
use most within it is Memcheck, which detects memory leaks,
use-after-free, uninitialized reads, and buffer overflows - it
catches these at the exact instruction that caused them, with a
stack trace, rather than letting the bug manifest as a mysterious
crash somewhere else later. The trade-off is it runs the program
10-30x slower, since it's emulating every instruction, so it's a
debugging/CI tool, not something you run in production."

COMMON USAGE:
    valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all ./my_program
    # --leak-check=full: reports each individual leak location
    # --show-leak-kinds=all: includes "still reachable" blocks too

    valgrind --tool=helgrind ./my_program
    # Helgrind: detects race conditions and lock-order violations in
    # multithreaded code - a different tool within the Valgrind suite

READING OUTPUT (say this if asked):
"A typical leak report gives you the number of bytes leaked, how
many allocation blocks, and a stack trace back to the exact malloc/
new call that allocated the never-freed memory - that stack trace is
the important part, since it tells you exactly where to add the
missing delete or switch to a smart pointer."

