B. GDB (GNU Debugger)
=====================================================================
"GDB - GNU Debugger - is the standard debugger for compiled
C/C++ on Linux. It lets you set breakpoints, step through code line
by line, inspect variable values and memory, examine the call stack,
and even attach to an already-running process to debug it live -
critical for daemons and services you can't easily restart with a
debugger attached from the start."

ESSENTIAL COMMANDS (know these cold):
    gdb ./my_program              # start gdb on a binary
    gdb -p <pid>                  # attach to an already-running process
    break main                    # set breakpoint at function main
    break file.cpp:42             # set breakpoint at a specific line
    run                           # start/continue execution
    next  (n)                     # step over (don't enter function calls)
    step  (s)                     # step into function calls
    continue (c)                  # resume until next breakpoint
    print variable_name (p)       # inspect a variable's value
    backtrace (bt)                # show the full call stack
    watch variable_name           # break whenever this variable changes
    bt full                       # backtrace with local variables at each frame

DEBUGGING A CRASH FROM A CORE DUMP:
    gdb ./my_program core
    bt                            # shows exactly where it crashed

Say this if asked "how do you debug a segfault": "First I'd check if
a core dump was generated, load it with gdb, and run backtrace to
see the exact call stack at the moment of the crash - that usually
points straight at the null/dangling pointer dereference. If no core
dump, I'd run the program directly under gdb and let it break
automatically on the signal."

