C. KDB / KGDB (Kernel-level debugging)
=====================================================================
Full forms:
- KDB = Kernel Debugger
- KGDB = Kernel GNU Debugger (also sometimes read as "Kernel-mode
  GDB")

SPOKEN ANSWER:
"Both are for debugging the Linux kernel itself, not userspace
programs - relevant when you're working below the level GDB alone
can reach, like your own kernel modules or drivers. KDB is a
lightweight, built-in kernel debugger - you interact with it
directly at the machine's console (keyboard/monitor attached to the
box being debugged), useful for quick inspection without needing a
second machine. KGDB is more powerful: it lets you connect a
*separate* development machine running regular GDB, over a serial
line or network, to debug the target kernel remotely - so you get
GDB's full symbolic debugging, breakpoints, and stepping capability,
but applied to kernel code instead of a userspace process. In
practice, KGDB is the one you'd reach for during actual kernel
module development, since it's far more capable than KDB's basic
console commands."

WHY THIS MATTERS FOR YOUR BACKGROUND:
"Since I've worked with eBPF and fanotify-based kernel monitoring,
KGDB is the natural tool when eBPF's own verifier/tracing tools
(like bpftrace or bpftool) aren't enough and I need to step through
actual kernel-side code path behavior directly."

