D. HOTP vs TOTP (One-Time Password algorithms)
=====================================================================
Full forms:
- HOTP = HMAC-based One-Time Password
- TOTP = Time-based One-Time Password

SPOKEN ANSWER:
"Both generate a short one-time code from a shared secret key, used
for 2FA - relevant directly to your SSH 2FA project. HOTP derives
the code from the secret key plus a counter that increments every
time a code is generated - so the server and device just need to
stay in sync on the counter value, incrementing together each use.
TOTP is essentially HOTP but replaces the counter with the current
Unix time divided into fixed windows, usually 30 seconds - so
instead of an explicit counter, both sides independently compute
'what time-window are we in right now' and derive the same code, as
long as their clocks are reasonably in sync. TOTP is far more common
today - it's what Google Authenticator, Authy, and most 2FA apps
use - because it self-syncs without needing counter-state
persistence, and a code naturally expires after its time window
instead of staying valid until manually invalidated."

THE ALGORITHM UNDERNEATH (say if pushed further):
"Both are built on HMAC - a keyed hash, typically HMAC-SHA1 for
these - applied to the secret key and the moving factor (counter for
HOTP, time-window for TOTP). The HMAC output is then run through
'dynamic truncation' to extract a short, human-typeable numeric
code, usually 6 digits, from the full hash output."

TIE-IN TO YOUR PROJECT:
"My 2FA-for-SSH work was a fork of google-authenticator-libpam with
a custom policy layer - that PAM module implements TOTP verification
against a shared secret stored per user, checking the code the user
enters against what the server independently computes for the
current time window, typically allowing one window of clock drift
tolerance on either side."

