E. SQLITE - BASIC QUERIES
=====================================================================
SPOKEN INTRO:
"SQLite is a lightweight, serverless, file-based relational
database - the entire database is just one file, no separate server
process, which is exactly why it's popular for embedded and IoT
applications like local device state or offline logging."

E.1 CREATE TABLE
--------------------
    CREATE TABLE users (
        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
        name TEXT NOT NULL,
        age INTEGER,
        department TEXT
    );

E.2 INSERT (add a row)
---------------------------
    INSERT INTO users (name, age, department) VALUES ('Samar', 28, 'Engineering');

E.3 SELECT (read data)
---------------------------
    SELECT * FROM users;                          -- all columns, all rows
    SELECT name, age FROM users;                  -- specific columns
    SELECT * FROM users WHERE age > 25;            -- filtered
    SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY age DESC;         -- sorted
    SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 10;                  -- capped result count
    SELECT department, COUNT(*) FROM users GROUP BY department; -- aggregation

E.4 UPDATE (modify existing rows)
--------------------------------------
    UPDATE users SET age = 29 WHERE name = 'Samar';
    -- WARNING: omitting WHERE updates EVERY row in the table -
    -- a classic, costly mistake to mention you're careful about.

E.5 DELETE (remove rows)
-----------------------------
    DELETE FROM users WHERE age < 18;
    -- Same WHERE-clause warning as UPDATE - no WHERE deletes everything.

E.6 ALTER TABLE (modify table structure, not data)
--------------------------------------------------------
    ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email TEXT;
    -- SQLite has limited ALTER support compared to other databases -
    -- e.g. it can't drop a column directly in older versions; you'd
    -- typically recreate the table without that column instead.

E.7 JOIN (combine rows from multiple tables)
--------------------------------------------------
SPOKEN ANSWER:
"A JOIN combines rows from two or more tables based on a related
column. INNER JOIN returns only rows that have a match in both
tables. LEFT JOIN returns every row from the left table, plus
matched data from the right table where it exists, and NULLs where
it doesn't. SQLite doesn't support RIGHT JOIN or FULL OUTER JOIN
directly - you'd simulate a RIGHT JOIN by swapping table order in a
LEFT JOIN instead."

    -- Example: users table + orders table, linked by user_id

    -- INNER JOIN: only users who have at least one order
    SELECT users.name, orders.item
    FROM users
    INNER JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;

    -- LEFT JOIN: all users, with order info if it exists (NULL if not)
    SELECT users.name, orders.item
    FROM users
    LEFT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;

E.8 Basic WHERE clause operators (quick recall)
------------------------------------------------------
    =, !=, <, >, <=, >=      standard comparisons
    LIKE '%pattern%'          pattern matching (% = any chars, _ = one char)
    IN (1, 2, 3)              matches any value in a list
    BETWEEN 10 AND 20         inclusive range
    IS NULL / IS NOT NULL     null checks (can't use = NULL)
    AND / OR / NOT            combine conditions

