F. REGEX - WHY USE IT (AND WHEN NOT TO)
=====================================================================
This connects directly to your Content Scanner CLI project, which
uses PCRE2 with JIT compilation - a great story to tell if they ask
"why regex instead of X."

SPOKEN ANSWER (why regex at all):
"Regex gives you a declarative, compact way to describe a pattern -
'find something that looks like an email' or 'match any line
containing a credit card number format' - without hand-writing a
custom parser for every pattern shape. For a content scanner
inspecting arbitrary file content for sensitive-data patterns, the
alternative would be writing bespoke string-matching logic per
pattern type, which doesn't scale as new pattern types get added and
is much easier to get subtly wrong."

WHY PCRE2 SPECIFICALLY (vs std::regex, Boost.Regex, manual parsing):
"std::regex, part of the C++ standard library, is convenient since
it needs no external dependency, but it's known to be significantly
slower than PCRE2 in practice - std::regex implementations
generally don't JIT-compile the pattern, they interpret it on every
match. PCRE2 - Perl Compatible Regular Expressions, version 2 - adds
a genuine JIT compiler: it compiles the regex pattern down to native
machine code once, then reuses that compiled matcher on every
subsequent scan, which is a large speedup for something like a
content scanner running the same set of patterns repeatedly over
many files. Boost.Regex is a reasonable middle ground, but adds a
Boost dependency without PCRE2's speed advantage. For a real-time
scanning daemon where I'm running potentially dozens of patterns
against every file event, that JIT compilation is the deciding
factor - it's the difference between a scan adding negligible
latency versus becoming a bottleneck."

WHEN NOT TO USE REGEX (worth saying to show judgment, not just
default enthusiasm for the tool):
"Regex is the wrong tool when the pattern isn't actually
regular in structure - e.g. matching balanced/nested brackets or
parsing a full structured format like JSON or XML, which regex
handles poorly or not at all; a real parser is correct there. It's
also overkill for a trivial fixed-string search - a plain substring
search like strstr or std::string::find is faster and clearer when
you're not actually matching a pattern, just a literal string. And
for extremely hot loops where even PCRE2's JIT-compiled matching is
too slow, a hand-written finite state machine for one specific
pattern can outperform a general regex engine, at the cost of losing
flexibility to easily add new patterns later."

CODE EXAMPLE (PCRE2 JIT compile-once, match-many pattern):
    #include <pcre2.h>

    pcre2_code* compilePattern(const char* pattern) {
        int errorcode;
        PCRE2_SIZE erroroffset;
        pcre2_code* re = pcre2_compile(
            (PCRE2_SPTR)pattern, PCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED,
            0, &errorcode, &erroroffset, NULL);

        if (re != NULL) {
            pcre2_jit_compile(re, PCRE2_JIT_COMPLETE);  // JIT-compile once
        }
        return re;  // reuse this compiled pattern across many match() calls
    }

    bool matchAgainst(pcre2_code* re, const char* subject) {
        pcre2_match_data* match_data =
            pcre2_match_data_create_from_pattern(re, NULL);
        int rc = pcre2_match(re, (PCRE2_SPTR)subject, strlen(subject),
                              0, 0, match_data, NULL);
        pcre2_match_data_free(match_data);
        return rc >= 0;
    }
    // Compile once at startup, call matchAgainst() repeatedly per file -
    // this compile-once/match-many pattern is exactly why PCRE2 JIT wins

