G. RESUME TOOLS - QUICK-RECALL GLOSSARY
=====================================================================
One-liner each, mapped to why you'd know it from your actual
projects - useful if they rapid-fire "what is X" down your resume's
tools list.

- Git: Distributed version control - tracks changes, branches, and
  enables collaboration across your security-tooling codebases.
- CMake: Cross-platform build-system generator - writes a single
  CMakeLists.txt that generates native build files (Makefiles,
  Xcode projects, etc.) per platform - useful when your tools target
  both macOS and Linux.
- Make: The classic build automation tool - reads a Makefile
  describing target dependencies and the commands to build them,
  only rebuilding what's changed based on file timestamps.
- Xcode: Apple's IDE - used for building/debugging macOS-targeted
  tools like your FSEvents-based File Integrity Monitor or the
  Printer Control/DLP app using Apple's Endpoint Security Framework.
- GDB: (see Section B above) - Linux/general C++ debugger.
- Valgrind: (see Section A above) - memory error/leak detection.
- KDB/KGDB: (see Section C above) - kernel-level debugging.
- SQLite: (see Section E above) - lightweight embedded relational
  database, useful for local state/log storage in your tools.
- pugixml: A lightweight, fast C++ XML parsing library - useful when
  a tool needs to read/write config or report data in XML without
  pulling in a heavier XML library.
- Boost: A large collection of peer-reviewed C++ libraries extending
  the standard library - things like Boost.Asio for networking,
  Boost.Filesystem (pre-C++17), or Boost.Regex, commonly used in
  systems-level C++ before certain features were standardized.
- Nano: A simple terminal-based text editor - quick edits on a
  remote Linux box or embedded target over SSH without needing a
  full IDE.

